  modelEntry: { s: ["mon", "mon", "monu", "monit"], p: "V", c: [2, 1], k: "TRANS" },
  intro: "<p>Second-conjugation verbs have a first principal part in <b>-eō</b> and an infinitive in <b>-ēre</b> with a long ē: mon<b>eō</b>, mon<b>ēre</b>. The present stem is <b>monē-</b>; unlike the first conjugation, the stem vowel survives even in the first person singular (mone-ō) because e does not contract with ō. Contrast the short e of third-conjugation regere: ē vs e in the infinitive is the only way to tell the two apart in print.</p><p>Perfects vary: the commonest type is <b>-uī</b> (monuī, habuī, tenuī), but there are also lengthened-vowel perfects (videō, vīdī; moveō, mōvī), s-perfects (iubeō, iussī), and reduplicated ones (mordeō, momordī). The supine stem is usually <b>-it-</b> (monitum, habitum) or shows the same change as the perfect (vīsum, mōtum).</p>",
  formation: "<p><b>Present system</b> on monē-: present moneō, monēs, monet, monēmus, monētis, monent; imperfect monē-<b>ba</b>-m; future monē-<b>b</b>-ō, monēbis. Present subjunctive adds <b>-ā-</b> to the stem: mone<b>a</b>m, mone<b>ā</b>s; imperfect subjunctive = infinitive + endings: monērem.</p><p><b>Perfect system</b> on monu-: monu-ī, -istī, -it …; pluperfect monueram; future perfect monuerō; subjunctives monuerim, monuissem.</p><p><b>Passive</b>: moneor, monēris, monētur …; perfect passive periphrastic monitus sum / eram / erō / sim / essem.</p>",
