  intro: "<p>Third-conjugation verbs end in <b>-ō</b> (not -eō) and have an infinitive in <b>-ere</b> with a <i>short</i> e: reg<b>ō</b>, reg<b>ere</b>. They are consonant-stem verbs (reg-), so a connecting vowel has to be inserted before the endings: reg-<b>i</b>-s, reg-<b>i</b>-t, reg-<b>i</b>-mus, reg-<b>u</b>-nt. This shifting vowel (i / u / e) is what makes the third conjugation feel irregular; the endings themselves are the same as everywhere else.</p><p>Perfects are unpredictable and must be learned from the dictionary: s-perfects (regō, rēxī; dūcō, dūxī; scrībō, scrīpsī), lengthened vowel (agō, ēgī; legō, lēgī), reduplication (currō, cucurrī; cadō, cecidī), or -v-/-u- (petō, petīvī; colō, coluī). The supine usually shows -t- or -s- (rēctum, scrīptum, missum).</p>",
  formation: "<p><b>Present system</b> on reg-: present regō, regis, regit, regimus, regitis, regunt; imperfect reg-<b>ēba</b>-m; future reg-<b>a</b>-m, reg-<b>ē</b>-s, reg-<b>e</b>-t, reg-<b>ē</b>-mus, reg-<b>ē</b>-tis, reg-<b>e</b>-nt (no -b-!). Present subjunctive takes <b>-ā-</b>: regam, regās, regat (identical to the future only in the 1st singular); imperfect subjunctive = infinitive + endings: regerem.</p><p><b>Perfect system</b> on rēx-: rēxī, rēxistī, rēxit, rēximus, rēxistis, rēxērunt; rēxeram; rēxerō; rēxerim; rēxissem.</p><p><b>Passive</b>: regor, reg<b>e</b>ris, regitur, regimur, regiminī, reguntur; imperfect regēbar; future regar, regēris; rēctus sum / eram / erō / sim / essem. The present passive infinitive drops -re and ends in <b>-ī</b>: regī.</p>",
