  intro: "<p>Fourth-conjugation verbs have <b>-iō</b> in the first person and an infinitive in <b>-īre</b> with long ī: aud<b>iō</b>, aud<b>īre</b>. The present stem is <b>audī-</b>. The long ī appears before consonant endings (audīs, audīmus, audīre) and shortens before another vowel or final -t (audiō, audiunt, audit). The conjugation is small but contains very frequent verbs: audiō, veniō, sentiō, sciō, dormiō, aperiō, inveniō.</p><p>The regular perfect is <b>-īvī</b> (audīvī, often contracted audiī) with supine <b>-ītum</b>; but veniō has vēnī, ventum; sentiō sēnsī, sēnsum; aperiō aperuī, apertum.</p>",
  formation: "<p><b>Present system</b>: present audiō, audīs, audit, audīmus, audītis, aud<b>iu</b>nt; imperfect aud<b>iē</b>bam (not *audībam, which is poetic); future aud<b>ia</b>m, audiēs, audiet, audiēmus. Present subjunctive audiam, audiās; imperfect subjunctive audīrem.</p><p><b>Perfect system</b> on audīv-: audīvī, audīvistī, audīvit …; audīveram; audīverō; audīverim; audīvissem.</p><p><b>Passive</b>: audior, audīris, audītur, audīmur, audīminī, audiuntur; audiēbar; audiar; infinitive audīrī; audītus sum etc.</p>",
