  model: { hw: "sequor", parts: "sequor, sequī, secūtus sum", gloss: "to follow" },
  modelEntry: { s: ["sequ", "sequ", null, "secut"], p: "V", c: [3, 1], k: "DEP" },
  intro: "<p>Third-conjugation deponents have an infinitive in bare <b>-ī</b> (sequ<b>ī</b>, loqu<b>ī</b>, ūt<b>ī</b>) — the shortest infinitive in the language and easy to mistake for a noun. Common members: sequor (follow), loquor (speak), ūtor (use + abl.), fruor (enjoy + abl.), fungor (perform + abl.), nāscor (be born), lābor (slip), quereor (complain), amplector (embrace), oblīvīscor (forget + gen.), proficīscor (set out), ulcīscor (avenge), īrāscor (grow angry).</p><p>The forms are the passive of regō with active meaning. The second person singular has the connecting vowel e: sequeris (present), sequēris (future), with the alternative ending -re (sequere) which also serves as the imperative.</p>",
