  intro: "<p><b>eō</b> is a fourth-conjugation verb with a one-vowel stem <b>i-</b>, which becomes <b>e-</b> before a, o, u: e-ō, e-unt, e-am, e-untis, e-undum but ī-s, ī-mus, ī-re, ī-bam. The imperfect ībam and future ībō are formed like the first and second conjugations (with -b-), not like the fourth. The perfect is iī (older īvī), with second-person forms īstī, īstis and infinitive īsse (or iisse).</p><p>eō is intransitive, so passives occur only impersonally (ītur, “one goes”; itum est, “people went”) — except that its infinitive īrī combines with the supine to form the future passive infinitive of every verb (captum īrī). Compounds are very common: abeō, adeō, exeō, ineō, redeō, trānseō, pereō, intereō, praetereō, subeō; transitive compounds (adeō, trānseō) do have a personal passive.</p>",
