  intro: "<p><b>volō</b> and its two compounds — <b>nōlō</b> (nē + volō, “not want”) and <b>mālō</b> (magis + volō, “want more, prefer”) — share an irregular present system built on the stems <b>vol-/vel-/vul-</b>: volō, vīs, vult, volumus, vultis, volunt; infinitive velle; subjunctive velim, vellem. The perfect system is regular: voluī, volueram, voluerō, voluerim, voluissem, voluisse. There is no passive, no imperative of volō or mālō, no supine and no gerundive.</p><p>nōlō: nōlō, nōn vīs, nōn vult, nōlumus, nōn vultis, nōlunt; nōlēbam; nōlam; nōlim; nōllem; nōluī; infinitive nōlle; <b>imperative nōlī, nōlīte</b> (used with an infinitive for prohibitions: nōlī timēre, don’t be afraid). mālō: mālō, māvīs, māvult, mālumus, māvultis, mālunt; mālēbam; mālam; mālim; māllem; māluī; infinitive mālle. All three take an infinitive or an accusative-and-infinitive, or a subjunctive clause (velim veniās).</p>",
  formation: "<p>Present volō, vīs, vult, volumus, vultis, volunt; imperfect volēbam; future volam, volēs; present subjunctive velim, velīs, velit, velīmus, velītis, velint; imperfect subjunctive vellem, vellēs …. Perfect voluī, voluistī …; pluperfect volueram; future perfect voluerō; perfect subjunctive voluerim; pluperfect subjunctive voluissem. Infinitives velle, voluisse. Participle volēns. Older forms: volt, voltis for vult, vultis; sīs = sī vīs, “if you please”.</p>",
