  intro: "<p><b>edō</b> is a regular third-conjugation verb (edō, edis, edit, edimus, editis, edunt; edēbam; edam; ēdī; ēsus) that also has a set of <b>contracted alternative forms</b> wherever the ending begins with s or t: <b>ēs, ēst, ēstis</b> (for edis, edit, editis), infinitive <b>ēsse</b> (for edere), imperative <b>ēs, ēste</b>, future imperative <b>ēstō</b>, imperfect subjunctive <b>ēssem</b> (for ederem) and passive <b>ēstur</b> (for editur). These look exactly like forms of sum except for the long ē: ēst “he eats” vs est “he is”, ēsse “to eat” vs esse “to be”.</p><p>The perfect ēdī has a long ē (edō, ēdī like legō, lēgī); the participle is ēsus (with the variant ēssus), hence the compound comedō, comēdī, comēsum / comēstum, “eat up”. An archaic present subjunctive edim, edīs, edit also occurs (in Plautus and in Horace’s edim).</p>",
